The '''global brain''' is a research hypothesis and metaphor in which globally connected humans, organizations and digital systems collectively process information in ways compared with a brain.

== Research context ==
Francis Heylighen and other researchers developed global-brain research around distributed communication, self-organization and networked problem-solving. The concept has historical parallels with the noosphere but places emphasis on the mechanisms of information exchange and coordination. Different formulations range from a design vision to strong claims about emergent global cognition; these should not be treated as one established theory.

== Functional modeling ==
A bounded model represents participants as nodes and communication as directed, time-dependent edges. Tasks propagate along permitted paths; nodes contribute evidence, decisions or computation. Compare distributed routing with centralized coordination using task success, delay, communication cost and resilience to node loss. A highly connected topology alone does not guarantee effective information aggregation; incentives, correlated errors and unequal access matter.

== Testability and limits ==
Claims about a particular global service can be assessed using observable performance and controlled changes in connectivity. Stronger claims that the entire planet constitutes a unified cognitive or conscious agent require independent operational criteria and evidence; neither large network size nor analogies to neural networks suffice. Governance, competing interests, fragmentation and infrastructure concentration limit the explanatory reach of the metaphor.

